  />

  <p>
    Rheumatoid arthritis is the big one on the anti-inflammatory side of the melanocortin
    story — the common disease that the rare-disease successes keep pointing toward. It is
    also where this field has most recently been told "not yet." Both halves of that are
    worth understanding.
  </p>

  <h2>Inflammation that does not end</h2>
  <p>
    In rheumatoid arthritis the immune system turns on the synovium, the thin lining of the
    joints. The lining thickens, fills with immune cells, and becomes a source of the
    inflammatory signals that erode cartilage and bone. It is chronic and systemic, and without
    effective treatment the damage is cumulative.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Modern treatment is one of medicine's quieter triumphs: start a disease-modifying drug
    early, measure disease activity, and adjust until it is low or gone. Methotrexate is the
    usual anchor, with biologic and targeted agents behind it.<Cite n={1} /> Nearly all of
    those drugs work the same way in principle. They find a signal that drives the
    inflammation and block it. That works for many people, not all, and it means dampening
    part of the immune system for as long as the drug is taken.
  </p>

  <h2>The other way to stop a fire</h2>
  <p>
    A healthy inflammatory response does not just fade. It is ended, on purpose, by a
    programme called resolution: the flow of new immune cells is halted, and the spent ones
    are cleared away by macrophages so the tissue can return to normal. This is where the
    melanocortin system comes in. <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc3r">MC3R</a> are found on immune cells, and activating them pushes
    those cells toward exactly that stand-down behaviour — the fourth job of the system,
    alongside pigment, stress and appetite, laid out on the{' '}
    <a href="/inflammation">inflammation</a> page.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The clue is old. <a href="/acth">ACTH</a> was used in inflammatory arthritis long before
    anyone knew it was a melanocortin, and its benefit was credited entirely to the cortisol
    it releases. The modern reading is that part of the effect never needed the adrenal at
    all. If so, a drug that activates the immune-cell receptors directly, and leaves{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a> alone, could keep the anti-inflammatory action without
    the steroid — a line of reasoning set out, with its sources, on the{' '}
    <a href="/inflammation">inflammation</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>Biased on purpose</h2>
  <p>
    There is an obvious catch. MC1R is also the pigment switch, and nobody wants an arthritis
    pill that tans. The answer is one of the more elegant ideas in current pharmacology. A
    receptor does not have a single output; it can signal down more than one route inside the
    cell. <a href="/resomelagon">Resomelagon</a> was developed as a <em>biased</em> agonist:
    in laboratory work it activated one route linked to pro-resolving behaviour without
    raising the cyclic AMP that drives melanin, and it did not induce pigment
    production.<Cite n={2} /> The same compound, in other words, is meant to ask the receptor
    for only one of the things it can do. Why that trade-off matters across the whole drug
    class is the subject of <a href="/effects">the trade-off</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>What the trials showed</h2>
  <p>
    The early signal was strong. In the EXPAND study, people with newly diagnosed rheumatoid
    arthritis who had not yet had a disease-modifying drug took resomelagon alongside
    methotrexate, and 82% reached an ACR20 response — the field's standard "at least 20%
    better" bar — against 52% on methotrexate alone.<Cite n={3} /> That was a small trial,
    about 28 patients an arm.
  </p>
  <p>
    The larger test did not confirm it cleanly. The Phase 2b ADVANCE trial, reported in 2026,
    missed its primary endpoint, with an unusually high response in the placebo group
    narrowing the gap.<Cite n={4} /> The sponsor points to a difference at the 40 mg dose, a
    significant effect in a pre-specified subgroup, and a clean safety record, and independent
    analysts have read the result as mixed and still worth a further trial.<Cite n={5} /> All
    of that can be true and the headline still stands: the trial designed to show the drug
    works did not show it. Subgroup findings generate the next hypothesis. They do not
    replace a met endpoint.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    The biology here is real and the idea is a good one. Ending inflammation through the
    body's own stand-down programme, by mouth, without a steroid and without suppressing
    immunity, would be a different kind of arthritis drug. But as of 2026 it is an idea with
    one encouraging small trial and one missed larger one, which puts rheumatoid arthritis in
    the mid-stage tier of the <a href="/conditions">conditions map</a> and not higher. The
    same receptors are being tested in the gut for{' '}
    <a href="/ulcerative-colitis">ulcerative colitis</a> and in the kidney for{' '}
    <a href="/membranous-nephropathy">membranous nephropathy</a>; the drug itself is covered
    on the <a href="/resomelagon">resomelagon</a> page.
  </p>

